Stop a subscriber sign-in from signing the operator out of the panel

A cookie is scoped to a host, not a port. The staff panel on 3010 and the
subscriber app on 5174 share one jar, and they also shared one cookie name, so
signing in as a subscriber replaced the staff session outright. Every panel read
then came back 403 for a Customer role.

The visible symptom was not an error message, it was a form that closed itself.
The panel treats a 403 on a read as a lost session and clears the user, which
unmounts the screen - so an operator editing a staff member had the modal
disappear as soon as they typed a character into the name field, and had to
reopen it. It was reported as "the field deselects after one letter".

Staff and subscriber sessions now use separate cookies, and each guard refuses
the other role outright rather than reading whatever happens to be in the jar.
Signing out clears both, since either app can call it.

verify:session-isolation drives the real sequence in one jar, the way a browser
does: staff sign in, staff read, subscriber sign in, staff read again. That last
step is the one that used to 403.

Also allows Vite's hot-reload websocket in development. The policy was refusing
it, so an edit was never picked up and the page had to be hard-refreshed by hand
- which is also how a stale bundle outlives a fix and makes the fix look broken.
Production is unchanged; there is no dev server to connect to there.

An earlier attempt blamed browser autofill and added a script to suppress it.
That was the wrong diagnosis, and it is removed rather than left in as dead code
next to the real fix.

Verified: typecheck and build clean, verify:session-isolation 8/8,
verify:write-authz 48/48 (including H1, a subscriber session attempting a write),
verify:auth 17/17, verify:read-path 8/8, verify:portal-isolation-live 9/9,
verify:write-queue 20/20, verify:fresh-subscriber 5/5, verify:password 5/5,
verify:sw 9/9.
